[49.835, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ I copied the document to `.ambition/first/url-shortener-architecture.md`. The project has no `.gitignore`,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  so I didn't add one.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Do you want to stop here or push it further?\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Stop here:** keep the document as it is.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Push it further:** I'd go after three things.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    1. **Check the numbers.** I'd run a small load test and a cache simulation with a skewed (Zipf-style)\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  traffic mix. That would test the 95% hit rate, the instance counts, and the ID-range runway.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    2. **Challenge the choices.** I'd question the 302 default, the key-value store choice, and whether the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  system needs this much infrastructure at 10k req/s. The honest alternative may be a much simpler setup.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    3. **Red-team it.** I'd go through abuse and failure cases, such as enumeration, phishing links, hot-key\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  storms and a Redis outage. Then I'd fold what holds up back into the document.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    That is a multi-step process of roughly 8 steps, with questions to you along the way. I'd estimate it at\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  several times what this first draft cost.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[54.245, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ Push it further.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[57.709, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ ▸ Step 2/8 · Altitude\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Question 1 of up to 4: what is this architecture ultimately for, and what should happen once it's done?\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **A. Build it.** A team will implement it, so the document should work as a spec they can start from.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **B. Design review or interview.** It will be defended in front of other engineers, so the trade-offs and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  rejected alternatives matter most.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **C. Capacity and budget decision.** Someone will use it to decide what to provision or spend, so the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  sizing and cost need to hold up.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **D. Learning or portfolio.** It is meant to show how you'd design this.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Something else.** Tell me in your own words.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[62.609, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ B. It's for a design review, so I'll need to defend it in front of other engineers. The trade-offs and the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  alternatives I rejected (e.g. key generation approaches, SQL vs NoSQL, cache strategy) matter most, and the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  numbers should be sound enough to back those choices up.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[66.932, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ Question 2 of up to 4: what would make this document unmistakably excellent in a design review? I mean a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  specific, hard bar, not \"good\". Pick the ones that matter, or set your own.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **A. Every major choice has its rejected alternatives.** Each choice (key generation, SQL vs NoSQL, cache\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  strategy, 301 vs 302) would state what it was weighed against, the numbers that decided it, and the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  condition under which the decision flips.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **B. Numbers that survive a skeptic.** Every figure would show its derivation and assumptions, and the key\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ones (hit rate, instance count, ID-range runway) would be backed by a simulation or benchmark I actually\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ran.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **C. Anticipated objections.** A \"hard questions\" section would answer the 10 to 15 challenges a sceptical\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  reviewer is most likely to raise, such as \"why not just Postgres?\" and \"what happens when Redis dies?\"\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **D. Readable in a meeting.** It would open with a one-page summary and a single clear diagram. A reviewer\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  could follow the argument in 10 minutes and look up detail afterward.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Something else.** Tell me in your own words.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[73.179, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ I'd go with A and B together, with D as the framing.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  A matters most to me. In a design review, people will challenge each choice, so I want to see what each one\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  was weighed against and what would make it flip. B backs that up: the key numbers (QPS, cache hit rate,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  storage growth, ID runway) should show their working and assumptions, so nobody can wave them away.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  D matters too. Open with a short summary and one clear diagram so I can follow the argument quickly. I don't\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  need the full list of 10 to 15 hard questions in C, but cover the obvious ones, like what happens when the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  cache or database goes down.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[77.254, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ Question 3 of up to 4: what would prove it, other than me? Pick whichever you'd trust in the review, or\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  combine them.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **A. Runnable checks, with the scripts kept.** I'd write a short script that recomputes the capacity\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  numbers from stated assumptions. I'd write a cache simulation using a skewed (Zipf) traffic mix. I'd write a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  collision check for the ID scheme and its scramble. You could re-run them live in the review.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **B. Reference figures from public sources.** I'd check the sizing against published numbers, such as\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Redis and DynamoDB or Cassandra throughput and the load figures published for large URL shorteners. Each\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  figure would carry its source.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **C. Both A and B.** The scripts test the logic and the public figures test the assumptions.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **D. You or a colleague checks it.** You review the numbers yourself and I only make them easy to audit.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  I'd recommend C. The review will likely challenge both whether the maths is right and whether the inputs are\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  realistic, and A and B each answer only one of those.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[82.053, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ C. I'd want the scripts so I can re-run the capacity and cache numbers myself, and I'd want the public\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  figures so the assumptions aren't just yours. Keep it light, though: a few small scripts and a short sources\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  list in the Markdown file, not a separate report.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[91.794, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ Question 4 is about missing access, and I'm skipping it. I can run Python locally and search the web for the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  public figures, so nothing is missing.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Here is the restated task:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ```\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  You asked for:   An architecture design for a URL shortener handling 10k req/s, as a Markdown file.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Raises all of:   - Architecture design: every major component stays, now with its reasoning visible.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - 10k req/s: the load model shows its derivation (peak vs average, read:write ratio,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     headroom), not just a stated ratio.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - Markdown file: it stays one file you can read top to bottom.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Genre bar:       A design doc that survives review by sceptical engineers:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - the argument is followable in ~10 minutes (summary and one diagram first)\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - decisions are justified against real alternatives\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - every number can be traced to an assumption\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - risks and failure modes are stated plainly, not hidden\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   It should read as a clear, confident engineer's document, not a catalogue of\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   technologies.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Aiming at:       A document you can defend in a design review, because the reviewers will\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   challenge each choice and each number.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Excellent means: 1. Opens with a short summary and one clear diagram.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   2. Each major decision (key generation, SQL vs NoSQL, cache strategy, 301 vs 302)\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                      has a short decision record: the options, what decided it, the rejected\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                      alternatives, and the condition that would flip it.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   3. The key numbers (QPS, cache hit rate, storage growth, ID runway) show their\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                      working and assumptions.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   4. The obvious failure cases (cache down, DB down, ID service down) are covered.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   5. Content that doesn't serve the review is cut. Deeper, not bigger.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Proven by:       - A few small scripts, included in the file, that you can re-run:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     capacity maths, a Zipf cache-hit simulation, and an ID-scheme collision and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     scramble check. I will run them and report what they return.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - A short sources list of public figures backing the assumptions.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - A comparison against the first version: it must be better on every part of the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     ask and worse on nothing. If a claim changes (for example the 95% hit rate),\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     I'll say so openly.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Needs access to: Nothing new (local Python, web search for sources).\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Mode:            build · you answer · no per-step review requested\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ```\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Two things to confirm:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - The scripts and sources go inside the same Markdown file, in an appendix, as you asked. I'll keep that\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  appendix short.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - I'll let the numbers change the design if they disagree with it. For example, if the simulation shows the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  cache needs to be bigger or the hit rate is lower, the document will say that, not defend my first draft.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Do you confirm this restatement, or want to adjust anything?\u001b[0m\r\n"]
