[274.011, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ I rewrote `<workspace>/url-shortener-architecture.md` for the design review. I ran the three scripts\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  embedded in it and they all work. I haven't load-tested anything, so the instance and Redis sizing still\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  rest on stated assumptions.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What the document now has**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - A summary and one diagram up front.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Short decision records for 301 vs 302, key generation, the datastore and cache strategy. Each lists the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  options, what decided it, and what would flip the decision.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - A failure-mode table covering cache down, database down and Kafka down.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Appendix A: the three runnable scripts with their real output. They cover capacity maths, a cache-hit\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  simulation and an ID collision check.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Appendix B: sources for the Redis throughput, DynamoDB quota and Zipf-like popularity figures, plus the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  gaps I couldn't source.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Where the numbers changed my first draft**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Cache hit rate:** my \">95%\" claim doesn't hold. The simulation gives 16–92% for a cache holding 5% of\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  links, depending on traffic skew, and 95% needs a skew of about 1.2 or higher. Published web-request studies\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  suggest 0.6–1.0. The datastore is now sized to take 100% of redirect reads, about 4,950 read units/s against\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  DynamoDB's 40,000 default quota. A dead cache costs latency, not availability.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Key generation:** random codes with a conditional insert replace counter ranges with a scramble.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Collisions are only 0.085% per insert at 3B links, so the ID service wasn't needed.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Sizing:** Redis drops to one primary plus one replica, and redirect instances drop from 8 to 3 for\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  availability. I also separated peak writes (99/s) from average writes (33/s). The first draft treated peak\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  as average.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Open assumptions**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - The 100:1 read ratio and 3:1 peak-to-average ratio have no public source.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - The ≥5k req/s per redirect instance is unmeasured.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - I found no published popularity exponent for short-link traffic specifically. Section 8 lists what to\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  measure first.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Compared with the first version:** the document is better on the decisions, the numbers and the failure\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  cases. I cut the \"future extensions\" list, and the first draft's endpoint list is now a short summary in\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Section 4. The copy of the first version is untouched in `.ambition/first/`, and a snapshot from before this\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  rewrite is in `.ambition/steps/2/`. The working copies of the scripts are in `.ambition/work/`.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  You said to finish without stopping, so I did the whole improvement in one pass. I didn't run the separate\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  later steps (for example a dedicated red-team pass) as their own stages.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
