[429.771, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ The architecture document is updated with two measured results. The fitted exponent in the first one came\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  out lower than I assumed, so the document now says 95% is not realistic. I snapshotted it to\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  `.ambition/steps/3/`. The embedded trace script reproduces its output when run from the document.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **The four statements**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **What a top practitioner would do that I didn't ask for:** test the two numbers the design leans on\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  hardest, instead of asserting them. Those are the cache hit rate and per-instance redirect throughput.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Rawest material:** I used a real hourly request-popularity trace, the Wikimedia page-view dump, 939k\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  pages and 2.76M views. A simulated traffic distribution was all I had before.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Instruments:** a Zipf fit and a stale-cache replay on the trace (`trace_fit.py`), and a bare Node\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  redirect server driven with `ab` (`redirect_bench.js`).\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Outside check:** the data and the load tool, not me. Anyone can re-download the same two files and re-\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  run both scripts.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What I found**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - The trace has a skew exponent of about 0.58 (about 1.0 only for the top 100 pages). A cache holding 5% of\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  pages hit 34–40%, which matches the simulation at skew 0.6–0.8. A cache filled from the previous hour lost\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  only about 1–6 points.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - A 95% hit rate is therefore not realistic for a small cache.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - A bare redirect path did 15.3k–16.6k req/s on one process, which clears the ≥5k per-instance assumption.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - That test left out TLS, the Redis hop, Kafka and logging. `ab` also shared the same four cores. The\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  document says so.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What changed in the document**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Section 2 now has a table of measured results, flagged as Wikipedia traffic rather than short-link\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  traffic.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - The Redis sizing rule is now \"hold the active set\". At ~200 B per entry, one 32 GB node holds about 150M\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  links. This is cheap, so Redis is sized by memory rather than by hit-rate targets.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - The flip condition for dropping Redis is tighter: if the hit ratio stays under about 30% even with the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  active set fully cached.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - New appendices A4 and A5 and a new source entry, plus matching updates to the summary, the open items and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  the changes table.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Changed course:** I dropped my earlier guess that real traffic is friendlier than the simulation. The\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  trace did not support it, so I removed that line. Short-link traffic could still be burstier than Wikipedia,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  and I couldn't measure that without real click logs.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[429.836, "o", "\r\n\u001b[33m⚑ Stop hook: Step 4 of pushing the work further: Challenge. Open your first message with this line on its own,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m  exactly: `▸ Step 4/8 · Challenge`. Write that line nowhere else.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  ⎿  … +15 lines of instructions\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[459.111, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ ▸ Step 4/8 · Challenge\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  First, the assumptions I'm testing, with who set each and what it depends on:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | # | Assumption | Set by | Depends on | Risk |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  |---|---|---|---|---|\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | 1 | The DB can take 100% of reads if the cache dies | me | per-table quota **and** per-partition limits,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  and no single key dominating | high, since the failure table leans on it |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | 2 | Peak:average write ratio is 3:1 | me | real diurnal and burst shape | medium, since it sets storage\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  and ID numbers |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | 3 | Read:write ratio is 100:1 | me | the product's usage | medium |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | 4 | An eventually-consistent read of ≤4 KB costs 0.5 DynamoDB read unit | me, from memory | AWS docs |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  medium; it was never verified |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | 5 | A 302 with `max-age=60` is actually cached by CDNs and browsers as D1 assumes | me | CDN and RFC\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  behaviour | medium |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | 6 | Random 7-character codes are fit to publish | me | what the codes spell | unknown |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | 7 | 10k req/s is a total peak | the request | the user's intent | can't be tested here |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  I'll test them riskiest first against real sources.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
