[541.084, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ The tests turned up two things I got wrong in the document and one blind spot. I've fixed all three and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  snapshotted the result to `.ambition/steps/4/`. The new embedded script (A6) runs from the document and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  reproduces its output.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What I tested, riskiest first**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Assumption | Test | Result |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  |---|---|---|\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | The database can take 100% of reads if the cache dies | Applied DynamoDB's per-partition limit (about\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  3,000 read units/s) to the real trace | **Holds, with a gap.** The top page carries 3.7% of views, about 370\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  reads/s, but a viral link needing ~61% of all traffic would exceed the limit. I cited that limit earlier and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  never applied it. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Read:write ratio of 100:1 | Searched for any public figure | **Not supported.** The only derivable number\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  is ~14 lifetime clicks per link from an undated Bitly infographic. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Peak:average of 3:1 | One week of Wikipedia hourly totals | The daily cycle is only 1.33:1. The 3:1 figure\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  has to rest on campaign bursts. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | An eventually-consistent read costs 0.5 DynamoDB read unit | AWS documentation | **Confirmed.** |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | A 302 with `max-age=60` is cached by CDNs | CDN documentation | Cloudflare and Google Cloud CDN follow\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  explicit headers. I couldn't confirm CloudFront from its docs. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | 10k req/s is a total peak | Can't be tested here | Left as your call. |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What changed in the document**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Ratio sensitivity:** a new table in Section 2 shows 5-year storage ranging from 0.3 TB (1000:1) to 17.5\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  TB (14:1). The design decisions all survive that range. The capacity figures don't, so the real ratio from\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  product data is flagged as the number to confirm first.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Hot key:** the failure table now states the per-partition limit and how the in-process cache bounds DB\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  reads of one key to about one per instance per TTL.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **CDN check:** D1 now tells you to send an explicit `Cache-Control` and test the chosen CDN with a real\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  request.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **New finding, the blind spot:** random codes can spell words. About 2.2% of random 7-character codes\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  contain a 4+ letter dictionary word, and 51% contain look-alike characters (`0 O o 1 l I`). I had no\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  profanity list, so this counts any English word, not offensive ones. The fix is free because the create path\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  already retries: reject blocklisted codes and generate again. D2 now says this, and says dropping look-alike\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  characters is a product decision.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Changed course:** I dropped the idea that the 100:1 ratio could be treated as given. It is now labelled as\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  unsupported and shown against its alternatives. The whole suite of scripts and the new evidence (hourly API\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  data, AWS and CDN docs) are listed in Appendices A6 and B.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[541.17, "o", "\r\n\u001b[33m⚑ Stop hook: Step 5 of pushing the work further: Range. Open your first message with this line on its own,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m  exactly: `▸ Step 5/8 · Range`. Write that line nowhere else.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  ⎿  … +15 lines of instructions\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[580.614, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ ▸ Step 5/8 · Range\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **The verbs.** The deliverable is still a URL shortener architecture document. What it has to do is:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Serve:** redirect strangers' clicks fast and reliably.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Protect:** keep it from being turned into a phishing relay or a way to leak private links.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Persuade:** convince sceptical engineers with arithmetic they can re-run.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Decide under uncertainty:** commit to a design while the key inputs are guesses.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Design and size:** the genre's own verbs, which stay.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Fields that do these differently**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Field | What it actually does | Direction I take |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  |---|---|---|\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Staff engineer (the genre's own) | Writes down non-goals, open questions and pre-mortems so reviewers\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  can't ambush the doc | Add scope and non-goals |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Site reliability engineering | Turns an SLO into an error budget and gives each hard dependency a share of\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  it | Derive which components may fail without breaching 99.95% |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Telecom traffic engineering (the unusual one) | Sizes capacity with Little's law and queueing curves, not\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  averages | Replace \"CPU > 60%\" and the connection counts with queueing arithmetic |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Trust and safety plus privacy engineering (red team) | Misuses the system as an attacker would and asks\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  who gets hurt | Attack the design |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **The edge.** A reviewer's hardest challenge to a design doc is \"what breaks this, and who gets hurt?\" The\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  edge I'm taking is that the document answers that with numbers before they ask it.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  I'll run the checks first.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
