[647.78, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ The document now has an abuse section built from an attack table, an availability budget, queueing\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  arithmetic and a scope section. A snapshot is in `.ambition/steps/5/`. The new script (A7) runs from the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  document and reproduces its output. I did the work myself and did not use subagents.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Fields and what I took from each**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Staff engineer (the genre's own):** a \"Scope and non-goals\" section. It names what is not decided\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  (multi-region, accounts and billing, custom domains, link editing, data-retention policy), so reviewers\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  can't use those as ambushes.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Site reliability engineering:** the 99.95% target gives a budget of 21.6 minutes of downtime per month.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  The redirect path has four hard dependencies, and each must reach about 99.987%. Six dependencies at 99.9%\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  each would give 99.40%, which is 12× over budget. That is the argument for keeping Redis, Kafka, ClickHouse\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  and the scanner as soft dependencies.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Telecom traffic engineering (the unusual one):** Little's law gives about 50 concurrent requests on the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  cache-hit path and about 297 if every read goes to the database. That sets the datastore client pool at\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  about 150 per instance after losing an availability zone. The queueing curve (1.5× delay at 60% utilisation,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  9× at 90%) is the reason for the \"CPU > 60%\" autoscale trigger. It rests on a simplified model, which the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  document says.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **The edge I took:** a reviewer's hardest question is what breaks this and who gets hurt, so the document\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  now answers it with numbers.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What the red team found**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Guessing:** a 7-character code is hit by 0.085% of random guesses at 3B links. A botnet at 10k guesses/s\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  would find about 736,000 live links a day. My earlier line that random codes make guessing hard was\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  misleading. People do shorten URLs that contain secrets, so the creators are the ones harmed. The fix is an\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  11-character \"unlisted\" tier and a statement that 7-character links are public by design.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Analytics privacy:** an unsalted hash of an IPv4 address can be reversed by brute force in about 0.5\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  hours on one CPU core. The fix is a keyed hash with a daily-rotated secret, plus a retention limit. I\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  proposed 30 days for raw events, as a placeholder for legal review.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Phishing relay:** the destination can be swapped after the creation-time scan. The fix is periodic re-\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  scans, rejecting chained shorteners, and no destination edits for anonymous links.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Header injection:** the user's URL becomes the `Location` header, so control characters must be\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  rejected.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Stale serving vs takedown:** serving stale redirects during a database outage could serve a link blocked\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  for abuse. I bounded the stale window and purge the CDN when a link is blocked.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Quota exhaustion:** mass creation needs per-account limits.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Changed course:** I dropped the 100:1 \"reads are cheap so abuse is mostly a rate-limit problem\" framing I\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  had implied. Guess density is the real exposure, so the first draft's one-line abuse list became an attack\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  table.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Not verified:** the GPU remark on hash reversal is general knowledge, not measured. The availability\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  numbers treat failures as independent.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
