[1067.884, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ The document now has a Section 9 on whether the same design reaches 100k req/s, and it does, with three\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  caveats. The outside check passes 104 of 104 (it was 94 before this step), and the snapshot is in\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  `.ambition/steps/7/`. The 10k design in Sections 1–8 is unchanged.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Target:** 100k req/s peak, 10× your brief, at the same 99.95% availability and p99 under 50 ms. The new\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  script A8 recomputes every number in Section 9.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Where the design breaks, in order**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  1. **The cache stops being optional.** At 100% miss the datastore needs about 49.5k read units/s, which is\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  124% of DynamoDB's default 40k table quota. The summary's claim that the DB can take everything holds only\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  up to about 80k reads/s. The quota is documented as adjustable, so requesting a raise before launch is the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  first fix. Without it, the cache would need at least a 19% hit rate just to stay at the quota, and Redis\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  would join the hard-dependency chain. I haven't confirmed AWS would grant a raise that large.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  2. **Redis must shard:** one instance would sit at 138% of the documented reference throughput, so I\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  specified 3 shards.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  3. **Redirect instance count is 12–34** (18–51 with one availability zone lost), depending on how fast a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  production instance really is.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  4. **The hottest key reaches 62% of a DynamoDB partition's ceiling.** That is based on the Wikipedia trace's\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  top page, not short-link data. The in-process cache becomes mandatory.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  5. **Analytics:** I proposed aggregating click counts in each instance and sampling full events 1-in-100, so\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  event volume stops tracking request rate.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What I couldn't do:** I couldn't generate more than about 15k req/s locally. A two-process run gave 14.8k\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  against 15.1k for one, which reflects this 4-core laptop being saturated by the load generator, not the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  code. The instance range is therefore bracketed by the 15k/s measurement and the 5k/s assumption, not\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  measured at scale.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Changed course:** I first meant to show multi-core scaling with a cluster run. When it came back\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  inconclusive I dropped that and reported it as a limit of the test machine instead of a result.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
